\section{Rules the entry point enforces}{

\code{static} is never implicit: a column of \code{targets} that is neither the response, the identifier
nor the anchor is ignored unless \code{static} names it, because a predictor nobody asked for is
worse than one that is missing.

One target row per \code{id}, unless \code{target_time} says where in time each row sits. Repeated
identifiers without an anchor are an error naming them.

With \code{target_time}, every representation has to be anchored on the target, so \code{\link[=native]{native()}},
\code{\link[=grain]{grain()}} and \code{\link[=multigrain]{multigrain()}} are refused and \code{sift} must be given as \code{\link[=lookbacks]{lookbacks()}}. There is no
default set of spans, because there is no defensible one.

Without \code{series}, \code{static} is the whole predictor block and \code{sift} is ignored.
}

\section{What a learner may be handed}{

A learner declares whether it reads a tabular block or a sequence. A tabular learner given
\code{\link[=native]{native()}} is refused before anything is built, and a sequence learner given a representation of
one bin is refused once the array says how many bins it has. Inside a \code{sift} expansion such a
pair is skipped and reported once by name; named explicitly through a learner's \verb{data =} it is
an error.
}

